      "name": null,
      "problem": "What is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{250}^{∘}$ expressed in terms of a reference angle?",
      "worked_solution": "To express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{250}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\nFirst, let's draw the angle ${250}^{∘}$ in the coordinate plane (CAST diagram):\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275599/assets/1ead1cacb3d5636bb0fc3f184966b10f1f83b58d512bd5e22cf8e0b589dd7bae.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta ={250}^{∘}$ is in the $3$rd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\theta -{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={250}^{∘}-{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={70}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{250}^{∘}$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{70}^{∘}.$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{250}^{∘}$ must be *negative* because the cosine ratio is *always* negative in the $3$rd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡{250}^{∘}=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{70}^{∘}.\n$$",
